  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1251 PM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nHigh pressure remains the main story today with light winds \npresent. There is quite a dew point spread across the region \nwith mid 50s in central Minnesota up to 70s down near the Iowa \nborder. Looking towards tonight into tomorrow morning a potent \nshortwave will track from the Northern Plains into the Midwest. \nIt will be mainly down in this region with the higher dew points\nacross southern Minnesota that will see impacts from the source\nof forcing. How far north rain will get will depend on the \nstrength of the LLJ we get and how much of a moisture push \noccurs. The Twin Cities metro is near the northern end of these\nchances that will be dependent on this additional push from the\nLLJ to see much chance for rain or thunderstorms. It will be \nthis area south that has the best chance. Overall CI is expected\nto occur to out west across South Dakota and will likely see \nupscale growth into an MCS as it moves east into southern \nMinnesota/Iowa. As is common with this convective mode, wind \nand heavy rain will be the main concerns as this passes through \ntonight or tomorrow morning. PW values will be up near 2 inches\nbased on CAM output, so heavy rain will be likely within what \nstorms do occur. It will be residence time of this rain that \nwill determine the flooding threat. \n\nTuesday will see more of a northwest flow pattern without much \nstrong forcing. Daytime heating will still be robust, as is \ncommon in the summer, and will provide a more airmass kind of \nthunderstorm environment. Shear doesn't look great for us and \nthat will limit severe chances such that SPC has marginal over \nus for tomorrow. In the SPC day 2 discussion they mentioned  \nthe chance for another round of upscale growth into an MCS as \none of the reasons for the marginal risk over southern \nMinnesota. \n\nCooler air and higher pressure look to be the story for the later \npart of the work week. This will see highs in the 70s with low PoP \nchances. Looking toward the weekend there is some signal within \nensemble systems for another round of rain. Scattering still \nremains fairly significant, but it is Saturday, especially \nearly, that shows the best chances. This is also reflected with \nsome likely PoPs in the NBM for this period. This does not look \nto be a strongly forced event, which introduces some additional \nareas of uncertainty. With these rain chances the cooler \ntemperatures should extend into Saturday, with some warming for \nthe start of the next week expected.",
